    "culturalContext": "In traditional Korean culture, rivers carry symbolic weight far beyond their physical form. With the influence of Buddhist cosmology, Koreans adopted the concept of Samdo-cheon (三途川) — the river standing at the threshold between the living world and the realm of the dead. The phrase '황천강을 건넌다' (crossing the Hwangjeon River) became common Korean shorthand for dying. The foundational Korean shamanic myth of Bari Gongju (Princess Bari) centers on the heroine crossing the underworld river to retrieve the water of life — the crossing itself is the defining act of spiritual transformation in Korean lore. The ancient Korean poem 'Gongmudoha-ga,' with its haunting line 'Do not cross that river, my love,' further enshrines the association between river crossing and the finality of death. In Korean folk dream interpretation, these cultural roots give the river-crossing dream its dual nature: clear water crossed safely by boat or bridge is auspicious, heralding a new life chapter with fortune and guidance; murky or violent water crossed alongside the dead is inauspicious, warning of danger, illness, or proximity to death. Shamanic tradition viewed the river in dreams as a mirror of the dreamer's spiritual condition and fate.",
    "westernContext": "In Freudian psychoanalysis, a river symbolizes the flow of repressed desires and emotions running through the unconscious mind. Crossing it represents a drive toward forbidden impulses or a rebellion against the authority of the superego — the psychic crossing of a boundary that the conscious mind has maintained. Clear water reflects emotional clarity; murky water, the depth of repression. The crossing becomes a charged act of psychological transgression or liberation.\n\nJungian psychology sees the river crossing as a central image in the individuation process — the lifelong journey toward wholeness. The river marks the border between conscious and unconscious realms, and between the current ego and a more mature Self. When this archetype activates in a dream, it signals a pivotal psychological transition underway. Jung interpreted the crossing as the unconscious drive to confront one's shadow and integrate it, moving toward a more complete and authentic self.\n\nModern cognitive psychology frames this dream as the brain's emotional processing of real-life transitions. People navigating career changes, relocations, or major relationship shifts frequently report river-crossing dreams. The limbic system translates the unresolved tension of waking-life uncertainty into the universal image of a threshold crossing. The water's condition and the difficulty of the crossing reflect the dreamer's internal assessment of how capable they feel facing the change ahead. Korean tradition and Western psychology arrive at this dream from very different frameworks — one reads fate, the other reads the psyche — yet both agree on the core truth: to cross a river in a dream is to stand at the edge of something life-changing.",
