    "culturalContext": "In Korean traditional culture, water has long been revered as the source of life, purification, abundance, and transformation. From ancient founding myths to shamanic practice, water carries deep sacred significance. The founder of Goguryeo, Jumong, was the son of the river god Habaek's daughter, while Silla's legends connect royal lineage to dragon-associated springs. In Korean shamanism, the Dragon King (용왕, Yongwang) was worshipped as the sovereign water deity governing seas and rivers, and coastal communities held elaborate rituals (용왕굿) to ensure safe voyages and abundant catches. Because water was understood as a dual force — both a divine blessing and a potential peril — dreams of falling into water carry layered meaning. Clear water symbolizes sacred purification, murky water signals contamination and misfortune, and the act of submerging then returning to the surface echoes the shamanic motif of death and rebirth. Just as overflowing water in a dream was traditionally seen as an omen of marriage or abundance, water dreams in Korean folk belief are frequently read as signals of life-altering change rather than simple bad luck.",
    "westernContext": "Western psychology's two towering figures approach this dream from different but ultimately complementary angles. Freud connected water to the prenatal uterine environment — amniotic fluid and the origin of life — making it a symbol of the maternal and the unconscious. Falling into water in a Freudian reading can represent repressed desires or unconscious conflicts breaking through to consciousness, particularly impulses to surrender control or regress to an earlier, less constrained state. Freud also noted that dreaming of being rescued from water by a woman specifically symbolizes birth and new beginnings.\n\nJung went further, declaring outright that 'water is the commonest symbol for the unconscious.' Dreams of falling into water, in his view, represent the ego being overwhelmed by the depths of the psyche — including repressed emotions, the Shadow, or collective unconscious forces. However, in Jungian terms this is not catastrophic: submersion can represent a necessary encounter with the unconscious as part of the individuation process. Emerging from the water then symbolizes psychological rebirth and a meaningful step toward wholeness and greater self-integration.\n\nModern cognitive science and neuropsychology take a more grounded view, interpreting these dreams primarily as the brain's emotional processing of real-world stress, anxiety, or feelings of losing control. Overwhelming workloads, interpersonal conflicts, major life decisions, or accumulated emotional pressure are all common triggers. Some researchers also note that physical stimuli during sleep — such as a drop in body temperature or changes in breathing — can generate a falling or submerging sensation that the sleeping brain weaves into a water-immersion narrative.\n\nNotably, both Korean folk tradition and Jungian psychology converge on a single key insight: surviving and emerging from the water represents rebirth, new beginnings, and renewal. This cross-cultural agreement suggests the symbolism taps something genuinely deep in human experience.",
