    "culturalContext": "In traditional Korean culture, lightning and thunderbolts were never regarded as mere weather phenomena — they were understood as direct expressions of heaven's will. During the Joseon dynasty, official state rituals were performed at the Pungunnoeu Altar (풍운뇌우성황산천단) in honor of the Thunder God (뇌신, also called 뇌공 or 뇌정신), reflecting how deeply thunder and lightning were embedded in the national religious consciousness.\n\nIn Korean shamanism (무속), the Thunder God's authority was embodied in the deity 구천응원뇌성보화천존 — the supreme figure of the sacred Okchuggyeong scripture — who was believed to use lightning to punish evil and exalt virtue. Against this backdrop, dreaming of being struck by lightning was interpreted as heaven's direct selection of the dreamer, imbuing them with divine energy — hence the dream's status as one of the greatest auspicious omens.\n\nAt the same time, lightning's capacity for sudden destruction meant that certain dream contexts were treated as warnings of unforeseen calamity. This dual nature is shared across East Asia: in China, the Thunder God (雷公) punishes the wicked and champions justice; in Japan, Raijin (雷神) is worshipped as a deity of abundant harvest and fertility. Throughout East Asian cultures, lightning has always occupied this paradoxical space — fearsome yet life-giving, destructive yet transformative.",
