    "culturalContext": "In Korean traditional culture, the rainbow has long been regarded as a sacred and mysterious phenomenon. Ancestors believed that heavenly maidens (선녀) descended to earth along rainbows to bathe in mountain valley springs — a belief rooted in the fusion of Korean shamanism and Taoist cosmology. The rainbow was seen as a bridge between heaven and earth, a passage connecting the divine realm to the human world. This symbolism is why a rainbow appearing in a dream was read as an auspicious message from the heavens. During the Joseon period, the practice of 홍점 (rainbow divination) was widespread — people read the direction and shape of rainbows to forecast weather and national affairs. The folk saying 'if a rainbow stands in the west, do not tie your cattle by the riverside' reflects this observational tradition. The rainbow's natural quality of appearing after rain also cemented its cultural role as a symbol of recovery and blessing after hardship — a core interpretive principle that carries directly into dream interpretation to this day.",
    "westernContext": "From a Freudian psychoanalytic perspective, a rainbow dream can be understood as an expression of wish fulfillment — the unconscious mind manifesting an idealized state of happiness or completeness that remains unmet in waking life. The rainbow's vivid colors and perfect arc may symbolize a longing for harmony or the projection of suppressed desires onto a beautiful, unreachable image. Freud's backward-looking approach would trace this dream back to early unfulfilled needs or fantasies of ideal circumstances.\n\nCarl Jung viewed the rainbow as a powerful archetypal image representing the integration of the Self and psychic wholeness. Just as seven distinct colors unite into a single harmonious arc, the rainbow symbolizes the individuation process — the journey toward integrating conscious and unconscious aspects of the psyche into a unified whole. As a bridge between sky and earth, it also reflects the collective unconscious archetype of a transcendent connector between opposing worlds, a symbol present across cultures and throughout human history.\n\nFrom the perspective of modern cognitive psychology and neuroscience, a rainbow dream is often understood as the brain's attempt at positive emotional processing during periods of stress or difficulty. The appearance of a rainbow may represent a cognitive shift — the mind reaching toward optimism and the expectation of resolution. Because of its striking visual quality, the rainbow is also one of the more memorable dream images, and researchers suggest it may reinforce positive thinking patterns related to hope, resilience, and the anticipation of change.\n\nBoth Korean traditional dream interpretation and Western psychology converge remarkably in treating rainbow dreams as symbols of hope and positive change. Where they diverge is in application: Korean tradition focuses on predicting concrete real-world outcomes — promotions, wealth, pregnancy — while Western psychology treats the dream as a reflection of the dreamer's inner psychological state rather than an omen of external events. The two frameworks complement rather than contradict each other.",
