    "culturalContext": "In Korean traditional dream interpretation, natural disasters have long been regarded as omens foretelling the fate of individuals and communities. Wind (風) held special significance in Korean shamanistic belief (무속 신앙), understood as a medium through which spiritual forces move and heaven's will is communicated to the human world. A typhoon — the most violent form of wind — was interpreted as signaling the most momentous fate-altering changes. During the Joseon Dynasty, severe winds and storms were sometimes read as heavenly warnings or divine retribution, and folk custom involved reading the aftermath of a typhoon — fallen trees, collapsed walls — to divine household fortune. Across East Asia, including China and Japan, typhoon dreams similarly symbolize violent change and chaos, with the dream's outcome determining whether it is auspicious or inauspicious — a framework shared across the region.",
    "westernContext": "From a Freudian psychoanalytic perspective, a typhoon dream represents suppressed libidinal drives or aggressive impulses breaking free from the ego's control. When the ego can no longer contain the overwhelming energy of the id, the dream borrows destructive natural phenomena as a release valve — connecting particularly to domestic conflict, rage against authority, or repressed tension long held in check.\n\nIn Jungian analytical psychology, storms and typhoons symbolize powerful archetypal energies from the collective unconscious — particularly the Shadow or Anima/Animus — erupting into awareness. The typhoon can be interpreted as part of the individuation process, dismantling existing psychological structures to allow new integration of the self. The calm that follows the storm is a potent symbol for the psychological maturity that arrives after integrating shadow material.\n\nFrom a modern cognitive and neuroscientific perspective, typhoon dreams typically reflect the brain's emotional processing of real-life stress, anxiety, or overwhelming change. During sleep, the amygdala reprocesses unresolved emotional arousal from waking life, generating extreme natural disaster imagery. People commonly report typhoon dreams during periods of workplace overload, rapid relational change, or major life decisions. While Korean tradition frames the typhoon as a fateful warning from spiritual forces and Western psychology reads it as inner turbulence, both traditions converge on one narrative: the calm after the storm heralds a new beginning.",
